{"type": "thinking", "text": "Budget sheet:\n- Title \"Total budget\" with edit pencil.\n- Big card: \"$60.5\" \"Spent\", \"Available: 40%\".\n- Black card: \"$100\" \"Total budget\", \"11 Nov → 04 Dec\".\n- \"20 Days remaining\".\n- Split: Daily/Weekly/Biweekly.\n- \"Calculated amount $1.9\" \"Tap for the full breakdown\".\n- \"Finalize budget period early\".\n\nThe currency formatting: \"$60.5\" and \"$100\" and \"$1.9\". Hmm. The change says totals formatted with selected currency. If the selected currency is USD, \"$\" prefix is expected. But the number format may be minimumFractionDigits? A currency format typically would show \"$100.00\", \"$60.50\", \"$1.90\". The app shows trimmed. Maybe that's the bug: amounts not formatted to 2 decimals.\n\nBut wait — maybe the actual bug is opposite: maybe they format with currency correctly, but the chosen currency in settings is something else. Let me check Settings. Also I need to check the \"Calculated amount\" breakdown by tapping. That might show amounts too.\n\nAlso \"Available: 40%\" - 39.5% rounds to 40% or 39%? Fine.\n\nPotential bugs to investigate:\n1. Budget sheet total \"$60.5\" vs expected \"$60.50\"? But the same format appears on the main screen and Analytics, so it's app-wide style. The changed note specifically says budget sheet totals now formatted with selected currency. So maybe test with different currency: change to EUR and see if symbol changes. Let's check Settings.\n\n2. Progress bar 0.15666668 seems wrong (maybe a bug but not in scope).\n\n3. \"20 Days remaining\" — precondition says period ends in 20 days, so 20 correct.\n\n4. \"Calculated amount $1.9\" = remaining 39.5/20 = 1.975 → 1.9? Hmm 39.5/20 = 1.975, displayed \"$1.9\" could be 1.98 rounded to one decimal... but displayed 1.9. Hmm, perhaps truncated or trimmed to 1 decimal? Actually if formatted to 2 decimals it'd be $1.98. They show $1.9, maybe the value is 1.9 exactly if calculated differently (40/21?). Let's see: budget 100, spent 60.5, remaining 39.5, days remaining 20 → 1.975 → displayed 1.9 (trimmed/rounded to 1 decimal?). Earlier before adding 0.5, spent 60, remaining 40, 21 days? The top initially said $1.9 (40/21 = 1.9047). Now after spending 0.5, remaining 39.5 over 20 days = 1.975, displayed 1.9? Hmm, both display $1.9. Maybe the displayed value is 1.90 (two decimals with trailing zero trimmed)? 1.975 → formatted 1.90? That would be wrong rounding (banker's?) 1.975 to 2 decimals = 1.98 typically. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe days remaining counts today as 20? Let's figure: period started 3 days ago, ends in 20 days (today + 20 = 21 days total). On the sheet \"20 Days remaining\". Daily = remaining/20. Before expense, remaining 40 → 2.0 not 1.9. But top bar initial said $1.9. Hmm, so perhaps daily = 40/21 = 1.90 exactly, and after expense 39.5/20 = 1.975, but displayed 1.9 means 1.90? Hmm, that's suspicious. Unless \"Calculated amount\" uses something else.\n\nActually maybe I mis-assume the initial remaining. Budget 100, spent 60. Daily = 40/21 = 1.9047 → displayed $1.9 — consistent with truncation to 1 decimal or 2 decimals + trailing-zero trim (1.90). Now after 0.5: 39.5/20 = 1.975 → display $1.9 could be truncation to 1 decimal or floor to 2 decimals (1.97?) no, 1.9 means maybe rounding to 1 decimal = 2.0 not 1.9. Hmm, so 1.975 truncated to 2 decimals = 1.97, displayed 1.97; but it shows 1.9. So maybe they use a formatter that only shows 1 decimal? Weird.\n\nHmm, maybe the number format is \"one decimal\" for daily calculated amount. Not necessarily bug.\n\nLet me focus on the changed features. The budget sheet \"amounts are now formatted with the selected currency\". Let's check Settings to see currency options, change currency, and see if sheet updates. Also test main screen number pad decimal key \"according to locale's separator\" — need to test with a locale that uses comma (e.g., change app language? maybe Settings has locale?). Hmm, device locale is likely en-US. Maybe there's a language setting in Settings. Let's explore Settings.\n\nAlso, \"Main screen category chip: applying a category now also updates the toolbar.\" I observed the Today pill color changed to olive after applying Food. Need to test more: apply Transport (different color), check the toolbar updates. Also after saving, does the toolbar keep the category color? On the budget sheet screenshot the top bar \"Today\" pill is olive, and after closing the sheet still olive. Hmm, after an expense was saved with Food, the toolbar remained olive. Is that expected? \"applying a category now also updates the toolbar\" perhaps means toolbar shows the category of the last applied? Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the \"toolbar\" is the top bar's Today chip which acts as a filter? Let me reconsider: In the initial state before typing, the top bar was a solid light-green bar with \"Today\" on left and \"$1.9\" right, no olive segment. After typing and applying Food, an olive segment appeared at the left containing \"Today\". So perhaps the top bar is a \"progress\" bar showing the remaining daily budget, and the olive segment is the portion spent today colored by the category? In that case after saving $0.5 Food, the olive segment should represent 0.5 of something. In the screenshot, the olive pill extends from x=44 to x=224 (about 180px of 838px ≈ 21%). Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe the top bar is literally the toolbar showing the budget progress: light green = available, olive = spent, colored by category? The Today label sits at the start of the bar with the category color behind it. That's plausible as a \"toolbar\" displaying category colors.\n\nHmm, but the change description \"Main screen category chip: applying a category now also updates the toolbar.\" I interpret: when you select a category chip (Food/Transport) on the main entry screen, the top toolbar (the \"Today\" chip) updates its color to the category's color. That's a cosmetic behavior. Potential bugs: selecting a category doesn't update toolbar, or toolbar color is wrong/category color mapping wrong, or after clearing/canceling, the toolbar stays colored incorrectly (state leak).\n\nLet me test: clear state now (input empty). The toolbar \"Today\" is still olive (Food color). If I select Transport, does it change to another color? But note the input is empty so chips aren't shown. Type an amount first.\n\nLet me test systematically after exploring Settings.\n\nActually let's check Settings for currency and locale options. Close sheet first (tap outside/Close sheet element 0 at 540,259). Let me just press back."}
