{"type": "thinking", "text": "The breakdown for Weekly:\n\"How it's calculated\nRebalanced daily · Weekly\nLeft of the total budget\n$100 − $0 = $100\nSpread over what's left of the period\n$100 / 24 days × 7 days = $29.17\nLeft of the total budget\n$100 − $61.5 = $38.5\nFor next week\n$38.5 / 17 days × 7 days = $15.85\nTip...\"\n\nInteresting! The main \"Calculated amount $29.17\" is the full-period weekly allocation: $100/24 × 7 = $29.17. Then \"For next week\" = $38.5/17 × 7 = $15.85. Hmm, why 17 days? Remaining after 24-7=17? Hmm. Actually perhaps: if period has 24 days and you choose weekly, current week's budget = 100/24*7 = 29.17; next week's = remaining 38.5 / 17 days * 7 = 15.85. Wait, 38.5/17 = 2.2647 × 7 = 15.85. Hmm, 17 days could be days left after the current week (24 - 7 = 17). That's plausible.\n\nBut then the sheet's displayed \"Calculated amount\" for Weekly $29.17 makes sense as \"the current week's allocation\" (fixed total split over 24 days × 7). For Daily, $1.9 = daily allocation, but the breakdown used $40/21 = 1.9 (remaining after yesterday) rather than $100/24 = 4.17 or $100/24 = 4.17. Hmm. Inconsistent: for Daily, it used remaining budget (after yesterday's spend) / days left; for Weekly, it used total budget / total days. Hmm. The Daily breakdown: $100 − $60 = $40; 40/21 = 1.9. For Weekly: $100 − $0 = $100; 100/24×7 = 29.17. Why \"spent $0\" for weekly? That's odd. Maybe it's the \"spending for the current week\"? Wait, today is Saturday Nov 14. The period started Sat Nov 11. Current week (first week of period) started Nov 11. Expenses Nov 13 ($60) are in the current week. So \"Left of the total budget $100 − $0\" with $0 spent is wrong — $60 was spent in the current week (Nov 13). Hmm! Unless \"weekly\" calculation is \"total budget / total days × 7\" ignoring spending, which is the \"Same every day\" mode rather than \"Rebalanced daily\". But the header says \"Rebalanced daily · Weekly\". Hmm.\n\nThen \"For next week: $38.5/17 days ×7\" uses spent 61.5 (all spent). So the \"current week\" calculation ignores spending while the next week uses it. That's inconsistent. Is this a bug in budget totals? It could be: the \"Calculated amount\" for weekly doesn't reflect spending (shows $29.17 while the \"next week\" is $15.85). Actually, maybe that's intentional: the current week's allowance was locked at the period start ($100/24×7 = 29.17), and next week will be rebalanced based on leftover. Hmm, plausible: \"Rebalanced daily\" means the current week's amount is set by start-of-week rebalancing. If the week started Nov 11 with full budget: 100/24*7 = 29.17 for the current week. Then next week uses leftover. That's actually coherent! The current week's amount was calculated at the start of the week (100 available/24 days), and next week's is projected. Hmm, but then the \"Days\" choice daily $1.9 used today's rebalanced daily (after yesterday's spend) rather than the current day's amount set at day start? Hmm.\n\nActually for Daily breakdown: \"$100 − $60 = $40\" (spent through yesterday), 40/21 = 1.9 — this is \"rebalance daily based on how you spend\" (as described in the radio option: \"Calculates daily based on how you spend. The remaining budget is spread across the remaining days of the period.\"). So daily rebalances each day. For weekly, maybe the week's amount rebalances at the start of each week: at period start (Nov 11), remaining 100 over 24 days → week amount 29.17. So current week 29.17, next week 15.85. That's consistent with the mode. Hmm, but then why does the daily tab show 1.9 rather than (100/24=4.17) at the period start? Because daily rebalances every day with latest spent. So both are \"rebalanced\" at their interval. OK, coherent.\n\nBut wait, \"Days remaining 20\" on the sheet and \"$1.9\" — using 21 days. Eh, \"spread over what's left of the period\" 21 days while sheet says \"20 days remaining\". Inconsistent counts: 21 vs 20. Hmm. The period: 11 Nov to 4 Dec = 24 days (11 Nov is day 1? 11→4 Dec inclusive = 24 days). Today Nov 14 = day 4 of 24 (as Analytics showed \"4 / 24\"). Days left including today = 24 - 3 = 21. The circle says \"20 Days remaining\" (excluding today). So two different counts, both labeled \"remaining\". Could be a minor inconsistency, but not necessarily a bug (day remaining could mean after today).\n\nOK, I'm going too deep. Let's get back to the currency formatting feature test. Close this breakdown: tap outside maybe. Earlier BACK exited the app. Let me tap outside the card, e.g., (540,200)."}
