arlier was #f4904b orange. So no.\n\nSo there is a bug: \"applying a category now also updates the toolbar\" — it doesn't in the preview state. But maybe the intended behavior is that the toolbar updates when applying the category (i.e., when the chip is clicked) — and it's evidently not updating. But maybe the toolbar updates only after a \"save\"? Let me test saving: type €0.5, Transport applied, tap check. Then the toolbar should show orange fill (saved Transport expense). That would show saving updates the toolbar. Then the \"changed\" feature might be about something else.\n\nHmm wait, wait. Let me reconsider the toolbar structure. Maybe the top bar is the \"daily allowance\" bar and the olive pill is not the typed preview fill — maybe it's a \"Today\" *chip* that gets the category color, and its width is fixed (a fixed pill, not proportional). Let me measure: In the current screenshot, the olive pill spans x=44..255 (211 px) out of the bar 44..872. In the earlier $1 typed screenshot, the orange filled region spanned 44..622 (~578px) and \"Today\" was inside it. So the width scales with the fill amount (or the chip width is dynamic). Hmm, in that $1 case, the orange fill = spent+preview? At that time today's spend was 1.5 Transport and typed $1 → (1.5+1)/1.925 = 130% > 100%, so why was the fill only 70%? Hmm, that doesn't fit. Let me recheck: the $1 screenshot was before I created the $1 expense... At that moment, today's spend was 0.5 Food (only the $0.5 expense). Typed $1 (preview). The bar fill orange (Transport? but the applied category at that moment was Transport — yes, I had selected Transport!). Wait, in that screenshot: \"Today\" in orange pill + $0.4 remaining. Fill 70%. Today's spend 0.5 + preview 1 = 1.5; daily 1.9 → 79%. Hmm 70% close-ish. Color orange = Transport (the selected category). So the preview DID recolor to Transport then! And the fill included the preview.\n\nSo earlier the preview recolor worked (Transport applied → orange). Now applying Transport → olive (Food color). Hmm! Maybe the order matters: I typed €0.5 (no category) → olive (default?); tapped Transport → still olive. Earlier: typed $1 (no category), then tapped Food → olive; then tapped Transport → orange. Hmm, in that sequence the toolbar did change to orange after tapping Transport.\n\nWait, did it? Let me recall the screenshots:\n1. After typing $1 (no category) at 6:55, I tapped element 5 (which opened the category edit field, not a chip). The screenshot after that showed the bar orange with $0.4 and the category field \"Transport\" focused. So the tap may have applied Transport AND opened the field. The bar turned orange. So yes, applying Transport recolored to orange.\n2. Then I tapped outside (which saved the $1 Transport expense?) and the toolbar showed \"Today $0.4\" with... the screenshot at 6:56 showed orange \"Today\" pill + pale $0.4. So orange persisted (Transport).\n3. Later, I deleted both today's expenses; the toolbar went all green.\n4. Now typed €0.5 (no category) → olive pill. Tapped Transport → still olive.\n\nHmm! So the difference: maybe tapping \"Transport\" the second time didn't apply because... the chip order: current applied category state? Before tapping, the chips were Food (left, 343), Transport (568), Category (864) — no category applied. I tapped element 5 = Transport at (568,1047) → after tap, Food (605) + Transport (862) → Transport applied (moved right). So it applied. But the color stayed olive.\n\nHmm, hold on: maybe the color didn't change because the bar color is determined by the *expense's* category, and the preview color is the last-used category color from the *last saved* expense. When no category applied, the preview might default to the last-used category (Food olive from the deleted expense). Applying Transport should override... it didn't.\n\nHmm, maybe there's an async/animation: the pill color update might be delayed/not applied when the toolbar is in \"preview\" state. Let me try tapping Food then Transport back and forth and screenshot each.\n\nActually, let me test with a fresh approach: type €0.5, tap Food (element 4 at 605 now) → screenshot; then tap Transport → screenshot. Compare pill colors. Let me do that."}
