{"type": "thinking", "text": "The breakdown now shows:\n- $100 − $60 = $40\n- $40 / 21 days = $1.9\n- Budget remaining $1.9\n- What you spent − $0\n- Total = $1.9\n\nConsistent with the toolbar \"Today $1.9\" and the sheet \"Calculated amount $1.9\". ✓\n\nAlso note the background shows the keypad in calculator mode (operators visible). The breakdown is a modal.\n\nNow, is there anything wrong? The line \"Budget remaining $1.9\" is actually the daily amount, not the budget remaining ($40). The label is misleading: \"What's left: Budget remaining $1.9; What you spent −$0; Total $1.9\". Here \"Budget remaining $1.9\" = daily amount; \"Total\" = today's remaining daily. Calling $1.9 \"Budget remaining\" is arguably wrong (remaining budget is $40). But the section title \"What's left\" may mean \"what's left of today's daily amount\". Hmm, it's confusing but probably intentional: the daily amount minus what you spent today = today's remaining. The label \"Budget remaining\" should probably be \"Today's budget\". Could be a wording bug but not clearly in the changed area. I won't report.\n\nNow let me close this sheet (tap outside) and check Analytics total ($60) and then sign off.\n\nActually, since the developer rejected the decimal report, maybe I should consider whether the duplicate bug is the only real issue. There might be another bug in the \"budget totals\" area that the changed build introduced: e.g., the currency formatting might be applied to the sheet but not to the *breakdown* in some currency? We saw € in breakdown. ✓\n\nWhat about the toolbar's remaining amount when currency is EUR: \"€0.4\" — symbol prefix. For EUR in a US locale, \"€0.4\" is the app's style. ✓\n\nLet me now think about the changed \"category chip updates toolbar\" feature once more. The tool description: \"Main screen category chip: applying a category now also updates the toolbar.\" We need to find whether applying a category fails to update the *toolbar* in some state. What is the toolbar? Let me look at the UI with fresh eyes:\n\nMain screen (keypad expanded) layout:\n- Top: budget pill (\"Today $1.9\").\n- Right: a calendar icon (\"Recurrent payment\").\n- Middle: amount display.\n- Chip row: [suggestion chips] + [Category field with tag icon].\n- Number pad.\n\nMaybe the \"toolbar\" is the top app bar that *normally* shows Analytics & Settings icons, but when you type an amount it shows the \"Recurrent payment\" button (calendar icon). Hmm, the \"toolbar\" could be that row (top right). Applying a category... updates the toolbar? No visible change.\n\nWait, maybe the toolbar is the row of icons at top-right for *Analytics/Settings*. Applying a category might update the... no.\n\nAlternatively, \"toolbar\" = the category chip row is a \"toolbar\" of chips. The change: when applying a category, the *toolbar* (chip row) updates to reflect it. Verified: the chip row updates (applied chip shows with tag, moves right; suggestions update). So the feature works. Are there edge cases where it fails? E.g., applying a category when the amount is 0 (no chips shown)? Not possible. Applying an already-applied category? Tapping the applied chip opens the edit field instead (inline edit mode). Hmm — maybe the applied chip should update the toolbar but instead opens inline editing. But that's by design per the \"Category list mode\" description (\"Tap the category tag to open a grid of your saved categories, instead of entering inline edit mode\"), implying tapping the tag normally enters inline edit mode. So tapping the applied chip is expected to edit.\n\nWhat happens if you type a *new* category name in the inline editor and press the check? The chip row/toolbar should show the new category. In our test, it saved the expense (duplicate bug) and the chip row hid. If we look at the next entry, the new category appears as a suggestion chip (Gifts/Test2 did). So the toolbar updated. ✓\n\nOK, I'm fairly confident the only confirmed bug is the duplicate save. The